ve points. So line y = -2x + n+2 covers about n/2 points.\n\nThus we can use two sunny lines: y = x and y = -2x + n+2. These are both sunny (slopes 1 and -2). They intersect each column at integer points for a up to floor((n+1)/2). Let's see coverage:\n\n- y = x covers points (a,a) for a = 1,..., floor((n+1)/2).\n- y = -2x + n+2 covers points (a, -2a + n+2) for a = 1,..., floor((n+1)/2). For a=1, y = -2 + n+2 = n. So point (1,n) is covered. For a=2, y = -4 + n+2 = n-2. So point (2,n-2) is covered. For a=3, y = -6 + n+2 = n-4. So point (3,n-4) is covered. So this line covers points along the \"other side\" of the triangle.\n\nThus these two lines together cover many points near the left side and right side of the triangle. The remaining points might be covered by vertical lines.\n\nLet's test for n=5. Points: (1,1)-(1,5), (2,1)-(2,4), (3,1)-(3,3), (4,1)-(4,2), (5,1). Use lines:\n\n- L1: y = x covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) (but (3,3) not allowed because a+b=6 >6? Actually n+1=6, so (3,3) sum=6, allowed. So (3,3) is allowed. So L1 covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Good.\n\n- L2: y = -2x + 7 (since n+2=7). For a=1, y=5 => (1,5). For a=2, y=3 => (2,3). For a=3, y=1 => (3,1). For a=4, y=-1 not allowed. So L2 covers (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered points: (1,1),(1,5),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,3). Remaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). That's 9 points. We have n=5 lines total, we used 2 sunny lines, leaving 3 lines (non-sunny) to cover the remaining points. We can use vertical lines x=1, x=4, x=5 maybe? Let's see.\n\nIf we use vertical line x=1, it covers (1,1)-(1,5). But (1,1) and (1,5) already covered, but that's okay. It will also cover (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). So vertical line x=1 covers all remaining points in column 1. Good.\n\nNow we have remaining points: (2,1),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). Use vertical line x=2? That would cover (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). (2,2) and (2,3) already covered, but that's okay. So vertical line x=2 covers (2,1),(2